g home  americans secretary of state anthony  blinken says he will discuss the deal  with his russian counterpart sergey  lavrov in the next few days it will be  the first conversation between the two  since russia invaded ukraine i plan to  raise an issue that's a top priority for  us  the release of americans paul whelan and  brittany griner who've been wrongfully  detained  and must be allowed to come home but as  of today a kremlin spokesman says there  is no deal whelan a former marine  remains in prison on what the white  house says are trumped-up charges and  greiner who has pleaded guilty is still  facing 10 years in jail for what she  calls a mistake  jennifer johnson global news washington  a private phone call from canada's top  cop made public  coming up would a senior rcmp officer  testified at the nova scotia massacre  inquiry we are coming to you tonight  from quebec city and we'll be right back  welcome back to quebec city and we are  here covering the latest in pope  francis's canadian tour and we're here  just steps from the notre dame de quebec  basilica cathedral where the pope met  this evening with local church leaders  the last event of this day now heading  into the final day of his canadian tour  we'll have more on his trip in just a  moment but turning now to other news we  are learning more tonight about the  compensation gun owners could receive  under the federal government's mandatory  buyback program a price list has just  been released outlining how much money  owners of banned firearms can expect on  the higher end a swiss arms sg550 could  net more than 6200 dollars while an  ar-15 would be bought for thirteen  hundred dollars  a senior rcmp officer says he believes  there was political interference in the  investigation into the deadly 2020 mass  shooting in nova scotia  chief superintendent chris leather told  a public inquiry in halifax that he  received a private phone call from rcmp  commissioner brenda lucky asking for  details of the killer's firearms other  rcmp members accused lucky of trying to  force them to reveal the weapons make  and model to help the federal liberal  government justify new legislation  so then is it your evidence today that  you are of the impression there was  political interference that's my  impression  the mass casualty commission will  release its finding in a final report  this november  the demands to change the game ahead  survivors speak out on hockey canada's  history of abuse  we're back from quebec city in just a  moment  reaction is pouring in from the hockey  world following this week's shocking  testimony by top hockey canada  executives on parliament hill abigail  beeman reports  my hope is that this is more like a me  too moment greg gilhooley is a survivor  of convicted sex offender and former  hockey coach graham james  he's one of the nine recipients of  hockey canada settlements totaling 7.6  million dollars the vast majority  connected to james he believes the  organization's maintenance of a fund for  victims separate from insurance is a  good thing but says it dropped the ball  closing the investigation into the  alleged 2018 sexual assault when the  victim didn't want to participate  further we still somehow find a way to  investigate a murder without you know  being able to talk to the dead person  many are unimpressed by hockey canada's  ceos testifying he plans to keep his job  including another james survivor sheldon  kennedy i'm prepared to take on this  responsibility  for change within our game i believe  i've got the experience to do it the  people who are making these  recommendations for change or promising  this change are the same people who  weren't worrying about making these  changes years ago they're the same  people who have been in power for years  issues of abuse within canada's game  aren't limited to hockey canada canadian  hockey league executives also testified  wednesday i would say since the  mid-2000s  the leagues are really focused on trying  to stamp out that behavior former nhl  player daniel carsillo takes issue with  that as one of the plaintiffs in a class  action against the chl writing in a  statement i disagree i believe that  there is a culture of hazing and abuse  in the chl systemic failures continue to  occur in protecting the children and  young people who play in the chl  while gail huley is most frustrated  hockey canada claims it wants to change  but doesn't want his help i was abused  by graham james and went through hockey  canada's process i would have thought  there would be a place for me with  hockey canada to help them do better  and i can't get my phone calls answered  he fears hockey canada is only focused  on doing the right thing so long as the  right thing keeps people in their  positions of power  abigail bieben global news ottawa  we're back from quebec city in just a  moment  we heard earlier about the push to  rescind the doctrine of discovery i  spoke with a mohawk human rights leader  about the doctrine and the pope's visit  uh mr kenneth dear thank you so much for  joining us here so we're in the the  final hours of this trip now he'll leave  quebec city tomorrow then head north  what do you make of what you've seen and  heard so far first of all  it's good that the pope came he came  here and to address the indigenous  peoples i think that that's always  significant he made a very good speech  his apology was was pretty good it could  have been better i i i  know they he still hasn't taken  responsibility for the to put a whole  church and it keeps blaming uh  individuals or groups you know and and  really it's this institution of the  church that that contributed to the  residential school uh abuse and and they  haven't acknowledged that so we're a  little disappointed in in that so but  you know time is uh still uh coming  we're not we're not stopping here and  one of the most common frames certainly  i've heard from people is that this is  you know they appreciate the words the  apology but they would also like to see  some action uh what would you like to  see on that well first of all our  delegation came here the hood nashony  external relations committee we came  here  not to ask for an apology for for years  now we've been on a campaign to get the  vatican to revoke  the people bulls that make up the  doctrine of discovery and uh because  these papal bulls is the basis of how  states have uh  claimed sovereignty over indigenous  lands and  and we need the pope to to clarify  change history because states still  today use the the doctor of discovery in  court cases uh against indigenous  peoples i gather you met the pope in  person in i think 2016. what are your  impressions of him  and and i guess his sincerity the way  he's handled all the pope  francis as an individual is a sincere  person but he also represents the  institution i think the words that he  use in uh in rome uh last spring i think  those were the lawyers that were talking  you know i think what he what you heard  uh here is more of pope francis a little  less of the lawyers however it's still  there they you know they still won't uh  the church itself as institution isn't  taking responsibility and that's the  lawyers talking you know so but it's it  was a step forward it was getting a  little bit closer and a little more  sincere and i think you could see a  little bit more into the into the heart  of pope francis how would you summarize  the way this trip has gone and the way  you know the indigenous people of this  country have responded to it there's a  wide range of reaction there are some  people who are ecstatic that he's here  and and they appreciate his uh his words  and his apology and there's other people  on the opposite end would uh the pope  can't possibly say anything that would  satisfy them and shows the depth of the  anger and and the mistrust over over 500  years uh uh that is really ingrained in  uh in in in our peoples about the effect  of the church on our people and how do  we move forward from this i mean what  happens after the apology the  replication of the papal bulls is a good  step would be a great step uh because  it's there's just so much uh  uh uh  damage that's been done to indigenous  sovereignty and self-determination and  also their spirituality that was taken  away from us an apology is just words  you know an apology doesn't mean  anything unless there's action uh onto  it many indigenous peoples uh the word  apology doesn't exist in their language  and it's it's what you're supposed to do  is make things right and that's what  we're trying to do is to make things  right as much as we can uh today and  into the future  kenneth dear thank you so much for your  very appreciate it  and that is global national for this  thursday good night from quebec city
 

